## Creating a new Site

A site is a reusable place record. Collecting events belong to a site, and
specimen capture records reach the site through their collecting event. Create
one site for each distinct camp, trap line, locality, or other place used by
your project.

{% steps %}
1. Navigate to the site page using the bottom navigation bar.

2. Use the `+` button at the top right corner to create a new site. 
    
    You can also use the three-dot menu icon and tap `Create site`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    You can minimize repeated entry by duplicating a site instead of creating a
    new one. Open the three-dot menu and choose `Duplicate site`. NAHPU copies
    the site information, but leaves the `Site ID` and coordinates empty. Enter
    a unique ID and add the correct coordinates before saving.

    {% /aside %}

3. Create a site ID. 



    We recommend creating a site ID convention for your team. An example of site ID convention for mammalogy fieldwork:
    
    - City, town, or village is ID-ed as it is. For example, the city of Bogor will be `BOGOR`. 
    - The camp site uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}-CAMP{camp number}` format. For example, camp 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango is `GP-CAMP1`.
    - The trap line uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{trapline leader initial}-L{line number}` format. For example, line 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-L1`.
    - The pitfall line uses `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{pitfall line leader initial}-P{line number}` format. For example, line 1 in Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-P1`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    For a project involving multiple devices, use the same `Site ID` for an
    identical site. This makes exchanged site and event records easier to match
    when they are imported into another project device.

    {% /aside %}

4. Add the Site leader
    
    If this is a field camp, you can put the team leader name. If you it is a trap line, you can design a person in-charge of that trap line as the site leader.

5. Add the site type

    The site type categorizes the site by its characteristics or use. Choose a
    value from the project's configured site-type list.

6. Fill in the geography details

    We recommend to follow your museum convention for writing the precise locality and remarks.

    {% aside type="tip" title="Writing remarks" %}

    If the site is a trapline, it is a good practice to explain how the trapline is run. 

    For example:

    ```text
    The trapline runs from Shelter I to Shelter IV along the trails betweeen the to camp sites.
    ```

    {% /aside %}

7. Fill in the habitat information

    - Type: The type of habitats: plantation, lowland rainforest, upper montane forest, etc.
    - Condition: The condition of the habitat: disturbed, undisturbed, minimal disturbance, etc.
    - Description: A brief description of the habitat. For example:

      ```text
      Dominated by oak trees. The ground was mostly covered by ferns, gingers, and oak seedlings.

      ```

{% /steps %}

## Site information

The site form records the place and the people responsible for it:

- `Site ID`: a project-unique identifier used by events and exchanged records.
- `Site leader`: the person responsible for the camp, trap line, or other site.
- `Site type`: the configured category for the site.
- Locality and remarks: precise locality text and notes about how the site is
  used or operated.
- Habitat type, condition, and description: the environment observed at the
  site.

## Managing sites

Open the three-dot menu on the site page for these actions:

- `Edit site`: update the current site and its coordinates.
- `Duplicate site`: copy the site details into a new record with a blank ID and
  blank coordinates.
- `Show QR`: display the site record for another mobile device to scan.
- `Export site`: save the site record as JSON.
- `Scan QR`: create or replace a site from a site QR code.
- `Import site`: create or replace a site from an exported JSON record.
- `Delete record`: delete the current site. A site cannot be deleted while an
  event still references it.
- `Delete all records`: delete all sites in the current project. Events that
  reference those sites must be removed first.

When importing a site, choose whether the imported record creates a new site or
updates an existing site. Review linked personnel and coordinates after an
import.

## Adding coordinates

You can add coordinate manually from your GPS device or use the device coordinate.

### Get coordinates from a current device

{% steps %}

1. Tap/click the `GPS point` button next to the `Add coordinate` button.

2. It will take you the add coordinate page with the most of the information already filled.

3. Fill in the rest of the fields. 
    
    See the [manually adding coordinates](#manually-adding-coordinate) section
    for details. Your GPS unit name should match the exact model of your
    device. For example: `iPad Mini 4`.

{% /steps %}

### Manually adding coordinate

{% steps %}

1. On the site page at the coordinate panel, tap/click on the `Add coordinate` button.

2. Enter the coordinate name.

    Keep the name short and informative. You can use the `Notes` field to add additional information about the coordinate. 

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    We recommend creating a coordinate name convention for your team. Example of coordinate convention for mammalogy:

      - `{siteID}S` for a coordinate taken at the start of the trapline, such as `KMHH-L1S`.
      - `{siteID}C` for the coordinate taken at the center of the trapline.
      - `{siteID}E` for a coordinate taken at the end of the trapline, such as `KMHH-L1E`.
      - If the trapline extends beyond the first start point, the coordinate at the new start is suffixed with `{number}`. For example, `KMHH-L1S2` for first extension, `KMHH-L1S3` for the next extension, and so on until the trapline are fully moved.
      - If the trapline extends beyond the first end point, the coordinate at the new end is suffixed with `{number}`. For example, `KMHH-L1E2` for first extension, and continue in a similar manner as the start line extension.



    {% /aside %}

3. Enter the latitude and longitude values.

    NAHPU currently supports decimal coordinates.

4. Add elevation in meters.

5. Select the datum.

    The default datum is WGS84. Check your GPS settings for the exact datum of your coordinates.

6. Enter uncertainty values in meters.

    Watch the uncertainty of your GPS. Some old GPS may not record the number. It is a good practice to take note of the uncertainty if you do not bring the NAHPU device with you to the coordinate location.

7. Enter the name of the GPS you use.

    Example device name: `Garmin 62sc`

8. Add notes.

    Add additional information if any.

{% /steps %}
